(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["xix-c"] = {"qs": [["Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory", "The optimal portfolio for an investor on the efficient frontier depends on:", ["The number of stocks listed on the exchange", "The custodian of the portfolio", "The fund's legal structure", "Her risk preference"], 3, "The investor's risk tolerance determines which efficient portfolio she chooses — the tangency with her indifference curve."], ["Concept of Informational Efficiency", "If a market is weak-form efficient, which strategy should NOT consistently earn excess returns?", ["Using inside information illegally", "Analysing unpublished field research", "Trading on past price patterns", "Investing in private companies"], 2, "Weak-form efficiency means prices reflect all past trading data, so technical analysis cannot consistently beat the market. Other information may still add value."], ["Types of Investments", "Which instrument gives its holder the right but not the obligation to buy shares at a fixed price in future?", ["A warrant", "A non-convertible debenture", "A commercial paper", "A fixed deposit"], 0, "A warrant is an option to buy shares at a set price. NCDs, CP and deposits are plain debt instruments."], ["Investment Strategies, Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "A manager plans to invest in a company where a sponsor affiliate is a promoter. This should be:", ["Disclosed and approved by investors as required", "Done quietly to save time", "Prohibited for all AIFs in every case", "Approved only by the affiliate"], 0, "Investments involving the sponsor's associates are related-party transactions requiring disclosure and investor approval under SEBI's rules."], ["Investment Strategies, Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "What responsibility do members of an AIF's investment committee who are not employees of the manager carry for decisions they approve?", ["Have no responsibility at all, as they are only advisers to the manager", "Must be SEBI employees", "Share responsibility for decisions they approve", "Cannot vote"], 2, "SEBI holds IC members, including external ones, responsible for decisions they take, subject to the regulations."], ["Investments Landscape", "Financial intermediaries such as banks and funds mainly help the economy by:", ["Fixing the prices of all listed securities on the exchange every day", "Eliminating all investment risk for savers", "Replacing the need for capital markets", "Channelling savings to productive uses"], 3, "Intermediaries pool savings and allocate them to borrowers and businesses, transforming maturity, size and risk. They cannot remove risk entirely."], ["AIF Structuring", "The contribution agreement in a trust-based AIF mainly governs:", ["The custodian's fees", "The investor's commitment and drawdowns", "The SEBI registration process", "The investee company's shareholding pattern after each investment"], 1, "It records each investor's commitment, drawdown mechanics, default consequences and other terms."], ["Taxation", "Business income of a Category II AIF is taxed at fund level. When that income is later distributed, investors are generally:", ["Taxed again at slab rates", "Taxed at double the fund rate", "Required to pay GST", "Not taxed again on it"], 3, "Business income taxed at the fund level is exempt in investors' hands on distribution, avoiding double tax."], ["Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory", "A portfolio lies below the efficient frontier. This means:", ["It is the optimal portfolio for all investors, whatever their risk tolerance", "Another portfolio offers more return for the same risk", "It has zero risk", "It cannot be improved"], 1, "Portfolios below the frontier are inefficient; one with higher return at the same risk exists."], ["Regulatory Framework", "A Category II AIF has a corpus of ₹500 crore. The sponsor or manager's minimum continuing interest is:", ["₹12.5 crore", "₹10 crore", "₹5 crore", "₹25 crore"], 2, "For Category I and II, it is the lower of 2.5% of corpus or ₹5 crore. 2.5% of 500 = 12.5, so the requirement is ₹5 crore."], ["Investment Strategies, Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "Which step usually comes immediately before investment committee final approval?", ["Exit", "Detailed due diligence", "Monitoring", "Distribution to investors"], 1, "Detailed due diligence findings are presented to the IC for final approval, followed by closing."], ["Fee Structure and Fund Performance", "Fund capital is ₹200 crore and total proceeds are ₹300 crore. The preferred return is ₹16 crore and carry is 20% with a full (100%) GP catch-up. The manager's total carry is:", ["₹16.8 crore", "₹16 crore", "₹60 crore", "₹20 crore"], 3, "Total profit = ₹100 crore. With full catch-up and enough profit, the manager ends up with 20% of all profit: ₹20 crore. Without catch-up it would be 20% × (100 − 16) = ₹16.8 crore."], ["Concept of Informational Efficiency", "For an AIF manager, the main practical implication of market inefficiency in private markets is that:", ["Passive indexing is the only possible approach", "All managers will earn the same returns", "Due diligence is unnecessary", "Skill in sourcing and diligence can add value"], 3, "Inefficiency rewards skill in finding and analysing deals, which is why manager selection matters greatly in private markets."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "Side letters must be handled so that they:", ["Are hidden from SEBI", "Override the PPM without disclosure", "Comply with SEBI's rules on investor rights", "Guarantee returns"], 2, "Side letters must fit SEBI's framework on pro-rata and differential rights and be disclosed as required."], ["Investments Landscape", "An AIF holds the following. Which one is a financial asset (a claim on another party) and not a real asset?", ["A warehouse used for storing grain", "Farmland leased to tenants", "A debenture of a listed company", "A toll road operated by a concessionaire"], 2, "A debenture is a claim on the issuer's cash flows, making it a financial asset. Warehouses, farmland and roads are real assets."], ["Investment Strategies, Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "A private credit fund's key risk control is:", ["Covenants and security on loans", "Daily share price tracking of the borrower's listed competitors", "Index replication", "Investing only in cash"], 0, "Covenants, collateral and regular monitoring protect a private credit fund's lenders against deterioration in the borrower's business."], ["Fee Structure and Fund Performance", "After the investment period, a fund charges 1.5% a year on invested capital of ₹400 crore. The annual management fee is:", ["₹4 crore", "₹8 crore", "₹6 crore", "₹60 lakh"], 2, "Fee = 1.5% × 400 = ₹6 crore. Fee bases often shift from commitments to invested capital after the investment period."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "Which investor is LEAST suitable for a Category II PE fund?", ["One needing the money within two years", "An HNI with long horizon and surplus wealth", "A pension fund with long-term liabilities", "A family office seeking diversification"], 0, "PE funds lock in capital for many years. An investor needing funds soon cannot accommodate the illiquidity."], ["Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory", "Using the same weights (60:40), if the assets' standard deviations are 20% and 10% and correlation is zero, the portfolio standard deviation is about:", ["16.0%", "12.6%", "14.0%", "10.0%"], 1, "σ = √(0.6²×0.2² + 0.4²×0.1²) = √(0.0144 + 0.0016) ≈ 12.6%. 16% is the weighted average, which applies only when correlation is +1."], ["Indices and Benchmarking", "Vintage-year benchmarking is important because:", ["Funds of the same year faced similar conditions", "All vintages perform identically", "SEBI requires one vintage per manager", "It removes fee effects"], 0, "Market conditions at entry strongly affect returns, so comparing funds of the same vintage is fairer."], ["Indices and Benchmarking", "Under SEBI's framework, performance benchmarks for AIFs are published by:", ["Benchmarking agencies using data reported by AIFs", "Each fund manager for its own fund, using its chosen index", "The stock exchanges", "The Reserve Bank of India"], 0, "SEBI's framework uses benchmarking agencies that compile data reported by AIFs to publish industry and vintage benchmarks."], ["Introduction to Capital Market Theory", "Which measure is most appropriate to compare a fund that is an investor's entire portfolio?", ["Treynor ratio", "Sharpe ratio", "Beta alone", "Dividend yield"], 1, "When a fund is the whole portfolio, total risk matters, so the Sharpe ratio (using standard deviation) is appropriate. Treynor suits well-diversified holdings within a larger portfolio."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "Why does SEBI require AIF managers' key personnel to hold NISM certification?", ["To give them tax benefits on carried interest they earn", "To ensure minimum competence", "To exempt them from audits", "To guarantee fund returns"], 1, "Certification ensures key investment team members meet a baseline of knowledge of AIF regulations and practices."], ["Valuation", "SEBI requires AIF valuations to be carried out by an independent valuer mainly to:", ["Increase reported returns so that the fund can raise its next fund faster", "Avoid audits", "Set tax rates", "Reduce bias in NAV and fee computation"], 3, "Independence limits the manager's influence on valuations that affect performance and fees."], ["Regulatory Framework", "The maximum number of investors in an AIF scheme (other than angel funds) is:", ["1,000", "200", "49", "No limit"], 0, "SEBI caps an AIF scheme at 1,000 investors, except angel funds, which keeps AIFs as private pools rather than public offerings."], ["Introduction to Capital Market Theory", "A fund returned 15% with beta 1.5 when the risk-free rate was 6%. Its Treynor ratio is:", ["10.0", "9.0", "1.5", "6.0"], 3, "Treynor = (15 − 6) ÷ 1.5 = 6.0. It measures excess return per unit of systematic risk."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "Full-ratchet anti-dilution protection means that if new shares are issued at a lower price:", ["The investor's conversion price drops to the new price", "The investor loses its preference rights and becomes an ordinary shareholder", "The investor must invest more money", "Nothing changes for the investor"], 0, "Full ratchet resets the conversion price to the lower issue price, strongly protecting the investor; weighted-average methods are milder."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "A strategic sale is often favoured over an IPO because it:", ["Gives a complete exit at once, often with a premium", "Always gives a higher price than an IPO", "Needs no negotiations", "Avoids taxes"], 0, "Strategic buyers can acquire the whole stake at once and may pay a synergy premium."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "Which party in an AIF provides independent assurance on the financial statements?", ["The statutory auditor", "The placement agent", "The investment committee", "The sponsor"], 0, "The statutory auditor independently audits the accounts and reports on their fairness."], ["Valuation", "A company's free cash flow next year is ₹8 crore, growing 5% forever. At a 13% discount rate, the enterprise value is:", ["₹100 crore", "₹61.5 crore", "₹160 crore", "₹105 crore"], 0, "EV = 8 ÷ (0.13 − 0.05) = ₹100 crore. ₹61.5 crore ignores growth."], ["Regulatory Framework", "The minimum investment per investor (other than accredited investors and certain employees) in an AIF is:", ["₹1 crore", "₹25 lakh", "₹50 lakh", "₹10 crore"], 0, "SEBI sets ₹1 crore as the minimum investment per investor, with exemptions for accredited investors and certain employees or directors."], ["Valuation", "A valuer applies a discount for lack of marketability to a stake because:", ["It cannot be sold quickly in an open market", "It carries more rights than listed shares and therefore commands a premium", "SEBI mandates a fixed 30% discount", "It is listed on an exchange"], 0, "An illiquid holding is worth less than an otherwise identical freely tradable one; the discount is judgement-based, not fixed."], ["Introduction to Capital Market Theory", "The capital market line (CML) plots:", ["Individual securities' expected returns plotted against their betas", "Bond yields against maturity", "Company earnings over time", "Efficient portfolios of the risk-free and market portfolio"], 3, "The CML shows combinations of the risk-free asset and the market portfolio, with total risk on the x-axis. The SML uses beta."], ["Indices and Benchmarking", "A long-short Category III fund should ideally be benchmarked against:", ["The Sensex alone, since it is India's oldest equity benchmark", "Bank fixed deposit rates only", "A benchmark reflecting its net exposure and strategy", "A gold price index"], 2, "A long-short fund carries lower net market exposure, so its benchmark should reflect that, or a blend of market and cash."], ["Indices and Benchmarking", "The Kaplan–Schoar PME ratio is greater than 1. This indicates that the fund:", ["Underperformed the public index on a cash-flow-matched basis", "Had zero returns", "Outperformed the public index", "Is a public fund"], 2, "A PME ratio above 1 means the fund's distributions and value exceeded what the same cash flows would have earned in the index."], ["Types of Investments", "A real estate debt fund lending to developers faces which key risk?", ["Project delays and developer default", "Exchange-rate risk on rupee loans made to Indian developers", "Dividend cut by listed companies", "Index rebalancing risk"], 0, "Real estate lenders depend on project completion and sales; delays, approvals and developer default are the key risks."], ["Fund Due Diligence – Investor Perspective", "ESG due diligence on a manager mainly examines:", ["How environmental, social and governance risks are handled", "The manager's personal tax returns", "Daily share prices", "The fund's NAV only"], 0, "ESG diligence checks how the manager identifies and manages ESG risks in portfolio companies."], ["Investments Landscape", "Which investor is typically best suited to commit capital to a 10-year closed-end fund?", ["An endowment with perpetual horizon", "A household saving for a car to be bought next year", "A corporate treasury managing its weekly payroll cash", "A retiree drawing monthly living expenses"], 0, "Endowments and similar long-horizon institutions can tolerate long lock-ups. Short-term or income-dependent investors need liquidity."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "A custodian is mandatory for an AIF mainly to:", ["Safekeep assets separately from the manager", "Generate investment ideas for the investment committee", "Distribute units", "Prepare the PPM"], 0, "Independent custody protects investor assets and provides a check on the manager."], ["Fee Structure and Fund Performance", "Why do investors prefer carry placed in escrow until they have received back their contributed capital?", ["It reduces the risk of needing a clawback", "It raises the manager's carry", "It removes the hurdle so that carry is earned from the first rupee", "It increases management fees"], 0, "Holding carry in escrow protects against later losses that would otherwise require the manager to return excess carry."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "The investment committee of an AIF approves:", ["Investors' KYC", "The fund's audit report", "Investment and exit decisions", "Fees payable to the custodian and fund administrator"], 2, "The IC decides on investments and exits in line with the PPM. KYC, audit and custody are other functions."], ["Investment Strategies, Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "Growth equity differs from venture capital in that growth equity usually invests in:", ["Idea-stage start-ups that are yet to generate any revenue", "Distressed companies", "Proven, revenue-generating businesses", "Government bonds"], 2, "Growth equity backs established businesses needing capital to expand, typically with lower risk than early-stage VC."], ["Indices and Benchmarking", "A 2020-vintage PE fund ranks in the top quartile. This means its performance is:", ["Above the Nifty 50 total return index over the same period", "Guaranteed for future funds", "In the best 25% of comparable 2020 funds", "In the top 25% of all AIFs ever"], 2, "Quartile rankings compare funds with peers of the same strategy and vintage; top quartile means the best 25% of that peer group."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "The compliance officer of an AIF manager is responsible for:", ["Monitoring compliance with regulations and reporting breaches", "Selecting investee companies for the fund's portfolio and negotiating terms", "Raising capital from investors", "Valuing the portfolio"], 0, "The compliance officer monitors adherence to SEBI rules and circulars and reports non-compliance."], ["Regulatory Framework", "A Category II AIF wants to borrow to make investments. This is:", ["Not allowed, except temporary borrowing within limits", "Allowed up to 2x NAV", "Allowed without limit", "Mandatory"], 0, "Category I and II AIFs may borrow only temporarily for operational shortfalls, within limits."], ["Concept of Informational Efficiency", "Strong-form efficiency implies that:", ["Only past prices and trading volumes are reflected in current prices", "Fundamental analysis always works", "Even private information cannot earn excess returns", "Markets are always mispriced"], 2, "Under strong-form efficiency, prices reflect all public and private information. Evidence of insider profits suggests real markets are not strong-form efficient."], ["Valuation", "Precedent transaction multiples often exceed trading multiples because they include:", ["Lower growth assumptions", "A marketability discount", "A control premium", "Tax refunds"], 2, "Acquirers pay a premium for control, so deal multiples are typically higher than minority trading multiples."], ["Concept of Informational Efficiency", "Why are small unlisted companies often considered less informationally efficient?", ["They are required to publish daily accounts", "Their share prices are fixed by SEBI", "Few analysts cover them and information is scarce", "They are traded on stock exchanges continuously with many market makers"], 2, "Limited disclosure and coverage mean prices may not reflect all information, giving diligent investors an edge."], ["Introduction to Capital Market Theory", "Risk-free rate is 7%, expected market return is 13% and a portfolio's beta is 0.8. Its CAPM required return is:", ["10.4%", "13.0%", "17.4%", "11.8%"], 3, "Required return = 7 + 0.8 × (13 − 7) = 7 + 4.8 = 11.8%."], ["Taxation", "Dividend income received by a Category II AIF is taxed:", ["At the fund level only, with investors receiving it tax-free", "As capital gains", "Exempt in all cases", "In investors' hands as dividend income"], 3, "Under pass-through, dividend income retains its character and is taxed in investors' hands."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "An angel fund primarily pools money from:", ["Retail investors through SIPs", "Banks for infrastructure lending", "Mutual funds seeking exposure to listed shares of large companies", "Angel investors backing early-stage start-ups"], 3, "Angel funds, a Category I VCF sub-category, pool capital from angel investors to fund early-stage start-ups."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "An AIF set up to invest in unlisted small and medium enterprises would register as:", ["Category III hedge fund", "Category I SME fund", "Category II debt fund", "A mutual fund"], 1, "SME funds are a Category I sub-category, reflecting policy support for smaller businesses."], ["Concept of Informational Efficiency", "Which is a behavioural bias that may cause prices to deviate from fundamentals?", ["Perfect information disclosure", "Herding by investors", "Zero transaction costs", "Rational expectations by all investors"], 1, "Herding — following the crowd — can push prices away from fair value. The other options describe idealised efficient conditions."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "A 'key-person event' typically triggers:", ["An increase in carry", "Automatic winding up of the fund without any vote by investors", "Suspension of the investment period", "Higher management fees"], 2, "When named key people leave, the investment period is often suspended until investors approve a solution."], ["Investments Landscape", "Private markets have grown relative to public markets partly because:", ["Stock exchanges have stopped approving new listings of small and mid-sized companies", "Companies stay private longer, funded by private capital", "Private investments are guaranteed by SEBI", "Public markets are closed to institutions"], 1, "Abundant private capital lets companies scale before listing, shifting value creation into private markets. Private investments carry no regulatory guarantee."], ["Taxation", "A Category III AIF trust with indeterminate shares may be taxed:", ["At zero", "Only on distributions", "At the maximum marginal rate", "At each investor's own slab rate on the share distributed"], 2, "When beneficiaries' shares are not determinable, trust income is generally taxed at the maximum marginal rate."], ["Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory", "Two assets with standard deviations of 20% and 10% have correlation of −1. A 50:50 portfolio has standard deviation of:", ["15%", "5%", "0%", "10%"], 1, "With perfect negative correlation, σ = |0.5×20 − 0.5×10| = 5%. A different weighting (1/3 : 2/3) could reduce it to zero."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "A term sheet is generally:", ["A fully binding share purchase agreement", "A SEBI filing", "Non-binding on commercial terms, except clauses like confidentiality", "A valuation report"], 2, "Term sheets summarise key terms before definitive documents; most commercial terms are non-binding except confidentiality and exclusivity."], ["Fund Due Diligence – Investor Perspective", "Why do investors check a manager's key-person and succession plans?", ["SEBI fixes the salaries of key persons named in the PPM", "Results may depend on a few individuals", "It affects tax rates", "It sets the hurdle rate"], 1, "Departure of key people can disrupt the fund, so succession planning and key-person clauses are important."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "A fund of funds investing in multiple Category II AIFs offers an investor:", ["Lower total fees than investing directly in the underlying funds", "Diversification across managers, with an extra fee layer", "Daily liquidity", "A guaranteed return"], 1, "FoFs diversify across managers and vintages but add a second layer of fees."], ["Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory", "A portfolio has 60% in an asset with expected return 14% and 40% in one with 8%. The expected portfolio return is:", ["11.0%", "10.4%", "11.6%", "22.0%"], 2, "Expected return = 0.6 × 14 + 0.4 × 8 = 8.4 + 3.2 = 11.6%. 11% is a simple average ignoring weights."], ["Concept of Informational Efficiency", "A share price rises sharply on the day of a positive earnings announcement and then drifts upward for weeks. The drift is evidence against:", ["Weak-form efficiency only", "Strong-form inefficiency", "The existence of stock markets", "Semi-strong form efficiency"], 3, "Prices in a semi-strong efficient market adjust quickly to public news. A slow drift after announcements is a known anomaly."], ["Types of Investments", "Which investment type is most associated with special situation funds?", ["Seed-stage start-ups that are still developing their first product", "Listed large-cap blue chips", "Government treasury bills", "Stressed and distressed assets"], 3, "Special situation funds invest in stressed loans and securities of companies under distress or restructuring."], ["Introduction to Capital Market Theory", "Multi-factor models extend CAPM by:", ["Removing the risk-free rate", "Using several risk factors such as size and value", "Assuming that all assets have exactly the same beta as the market", "Ignoring market risk"], 1, "Factor models explain returns with several systematic factors beyond the market, such as size, value and momentum."], ["Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory", "Diversification is most effective when assets have:", ["Perfect positive correlation", "Identical returns at all times", "Low or negative correlation", "The same issuer"], 2, "Lower correlation means assets do not move together, so combining them reduces portfolio risk."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "A put option negotiated by a PE investor against promoters allows the investor to:", ["Sell its shares to the promoters on specified terms", "Buy more shares at a discount", "Force a merger", "Avoid all taxes"], 0, "A put gives an exit by requiring promoters to buy the investor's shares, subject to legal limits on assured returns for foreign investors."], ["Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory", "A key limitation of MPT when applied to private equity is that:", ["Private equity returns are reported daily", "Private equity has no correlation data at all, so no optimisation is possible", "MPT works only for bonds", "Reported returns are smoothed, understating risk"], 3, "Appraisal-based valuations smooth returns, lowering measured volatility and correlation, so optimisers may over-allocate to private assets."], ["AIF Structuring", "Why might a fund choose a company structure despite trusts being more common?", ["Companies pay no tax", "Companies need no SEBI registration", "Companies can raise from retail investors freely", "Some investors prefer corporate governance"], 3, "Some investors prefer board governance and familiarity of corporate forms, though tax treatment and flexibility differ."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "A fund focusing on companies creating measurable social benefits, such as affordable healthcare, would be a:", ["Category III long-short fund", "Social impact fund under Category I", "Category II real estate fund investing in completed office buildings", "Portfolio management service"], 1, "Social impact funds, part of Category I, invest in entities with social objectives and may raise money through the social stock exchange route."], ["Valuation", "The 'price of recent investment' method is most reliable when:", ["The round was two years ago", "The price was set by insiders only", "The company has since failed to meet its milestones and is raising a down round", "The round was recent and at arm's length"], 3, "A recent arm's-length round is good evidence of fair value; older or insider-only rounds are weaker evidence."], ["Investments Landscape", "An investor's required return on a private fund should be higher than on a comparable listed fund mainly because of:", ["Lower fees in private funds", "Illiquidity and higher uncertainty", "Daily liquidity in private funds", "Government guarantees on private funds"], 1, "Private funds are illiquid, opaque and costly, so investors demand a premium over comparable public investments."], ["Indices and Benchmarking", "Peer-group benchmarks for private funds can suffer from:", ["Too much daily data", "Exchange-traded pricing that makes every fund's NAV observable daily", "Survivorship and self-reporting bias", "Mandatory government audit"], 2, "If poorly performing funds stop reporting or disappear, peer data can be biased upward."], ["Valuation", "Calibration in private company valuation means:", ["Using the same value every year", "Taking the average of all analyst targets", "Using book value only", "Aligning model inputs with the entry price, then updating"], 3, "Calibration checks that the method reproduces the transaction price at entry, and then updates inputs as conditions change."], ["Investment Strategies, Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "A leveraged buyout typically creates value through:", ["Holding minority stakes passively and relying on the promoter to create value", "Investing only in listed shares", "Operational improvement, debt paydown and multiple expansion", "Avoiding any use of debt"], 2, "Buyouts acquire control, improve operations, use cash flows to repay acquisition debt and may exit at higher multiples."], ["Fund Due Diligence – Investor Perspective", "Which is the strongest sign of alignment in a fund?", ["High management fees charged on total commitments for the fund's full life", "A significant GP commitment from personal funds", "Guaranteed carry", "No clawback provision"], 1, "Personal capital at risk aligns the manager's outcome with investors'; high fees and guaranteed carry weaken alignment."], ["Investment Strategies, Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "A manager's two funds both want to invest in the same deal. Good governance requires:", ["Giving it to the fund paying more carry", "Letting the deal team decide informally", "Splitting it without any documentation", "Allocating per a disclosed allocation policy"], 3, "Detailed due diligence findings are presented to the investment committee for final approval, after which the deal moves to documentation and closing."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "A placement agent's main role is to:", ["Manage the portfolio and take investment and exit decisions", "Audit the accounts", "Introduce the fund to potential investors", "Hold the securities"], 2, "Placement agents help raise capital by connecting the fund with investors, for which they receive a fee."], ["Types of Investments", "Venture debt is typically provided to:", ["Mature companies with strong cash flows only", "Governments for infrastructure", "Retail investors for personal loans", "VC-backed start-ups to extend runway"], 3, "Venture debt supplements equity in VC-funded companies, often with warrants, helping them extend runway without immediate dilution."], ["Investments Landscape", "A fund earns a nominal return of 12% when inflation is 5%. Using the exact Fisher relationship, the real return is about:", ["7.00%", "17.60%", "5.71%", "6.67%"], 3, "Real return = 1.12 ÷ 1.05 − 1 ≈ 6.67%. Subtracting inflation directly gives the 7% approximation."], ["AIF Structuring", "An offshore feeder fund investing into an Indian AIF helps foreign investors mainly by:", ["Avoiding Indian KYC norms", "Pooling them in a familiar vehicle", "Guaranteeing a currency hedge on all rupee-denominated investments", "Allowing daily redemptions"], 1, "Feeders pool foreign investors in a jurisdiction and vehicle they are comfortable with, simplifying participation."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "An MFN clause in a side letter entitles an investor to:", ["Manage the fund", "Better terms later given to comparable investors", "Exit at NAV at any time, irrespective of the fund's lock-in terms", "Waive all fees"], 1, "MFN lets the investor elect to receive more favourable terms offered to other similar investors."], ["Taxation", "Losses of a Category I or II AIF (other than business losses) can be passed to investors only if:", ["They are non-residents", "The fund is open-ended", "The loss exceeds ₹1 crore", "They have held units for the prescribed minimum period"], 3, "Loss pass-through requires investors to have held units for at least the prescribed period, preventing loss trading."], ["Indices and Benchmarking", "Which statistic best measures a Category III fund's consistency of outperformance over its benchmark?", ["Dividend yield", "Information ratio", "Distributions to paid-in capital (DPI)", "Vintage year"], 1, "The information ratio divides active return by tracking error, measuring consistency of outperformance."], ["Fee Structure and Fund Performance", "Using the same fund (capital ₹200 crore, proceeds ₹300 crore, preferred return ₹16 crore, 20% carry) but with no catch-up, the carry is:", ["₹20 crore", "₹16.8 crore", "₹3.2 crore", "₹13.6 crore"], 1, "Profit above the hurdle = 100 − 16 = ₹84 crore. Carry = 20% × 84 = ₹16.8 crore."], ["AIF Structuring", "Differential rights for certain investors in an AIF are allowed when:", ["They follow SEBI's framework and do not harm others", "They are kept secret from SEBI", "They guarantee returns to those investors", "They are approved only by the manager"], 0, "SEBI permits differential rights within its framework, with disclosure, provided other investors' rights are not adversely affected."], ["Introduction to Capital Market Theory", "A fund returned 16% with beta 1.2. The risk-free rate was 6% and the market returned 12%. Its Jensen's alpha is:", ["+4.0%", "+2.8%", "+10.0%", "−2.8%"], 1, "CAPM return = 6 + 1.2 × 6 = 13.2%. Alpha = 16 − 13.2 = +2.8%. +4% compares with the market return without adjusting for beta."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "Which party must file the PPM of a new AIF scheme with SEBI?", ["A SEBI-registered merchant banker", "The investee company", "The custodian", "The investor with the largest commitment in the scheme"], 0, "SEBI requires the PPM to be filed through a merchant banker who conducts due diligence on disclosures."], ["Fund Due Diligence – Investor Perspective", "When reviewing a track record, an investor should prefer evidence from:", ["Unrealised marks set by the manager", "Projected returns in marketing material", "Industry averages", "Realised exits with cash returned"], 3, "Realised returns are confirmed by cash, while unrealised marks depend on valuation judgement."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "A buyback exit involves:", ["The company or promoters repurchasing the fund's shares", "An IPO", "A strategic merger", "A write-off"], 0, "In a buyback, the investee or promoters buy back the investor's stake, often under negotiated put rights."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Which of the following must an AIF manager do under the PMLA framework?", ["Accept cash freely", "Skip KYC for HNIs", "Share data publicly", "Verify investor identity and report suspicious transactions"], 3, "AIFs must perform KYC, maintain records and report suspicious transactions as reporting entities under the PMLA framework."], ["Valuation", "In a DCF, a higher discount rate, other things equal, will:", ["Raise the valuation", "Have no effect", "Double the terminal value", "Lower the valuation"], 3, "A higher discount rate reduces the present value of future cash flows."], ["Fee Structure and Fund Performance", "Fund expenses such as audit and legal fees charged to the fund mainly affect:", ["Net returns to investors", "Only the manager's income", "Gross IRR only", "The fund's tax status"], 0, "Fund expenses are borne by investors and reduce net returns, widening the gap between gross and net IRR."], ["Types of Investments", "A compulsorily convertible preference share (CCPS) is popular with Indian PE investors mainly because it:", ["Offers preference rights and later converts to equity", "Pays a fixed coupon that must be paid in cash even in years when the company makes losses", "Can never be converted into equity shares", "Carries no rights in a liquidation"], 0, "CCPS offers preferential rights, such as liquidation preference, and converts into equity, which is often favoured under FEMA for foreign investors."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "Which report helps an investor see its share of fund NAV and cash flows?", ["Private placement memorandum", "Trust deed", "Term sheet", "Capital account statement"], 3, "The capital account statement shows contributions, distributions and the investor's share of NAV."], ["Concept of Informational Efficiency", "An investor consistently earns excess returns using only published financial statements. This would contradict:", ["Weak-form efficiency only", "Semi-strong form efficiency", "The random walk of past prices only", "No form of efficiency"], 1, "Published statements are public information; persistent excess returns from analysing them contradict semi-strong efficiency."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "In-specie distribution at the end of a fund's tenure means:", ["Paying cash only", "Distributing unsold investments to investors directly", "Converting the fund to a mutual fund", "Selling all assets to the sponsor"], 1, "Investors receive the remaining securities themselves instead of cash, subject to approval."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "Why are Category I and II AIFs required to be close-ended?", ["SEBI wants them to trade on stock exchanges like closed-end funds", "Their illiquid assets cannot meet redemptions", "They hold only cash", "Investors prefer daily NAV"], 1, "Unlisted holdings cannot be sold quickly, so allowing redemptions would harm remaining investors."], ["Types of Investments", "Infrastructure investments are attractive to long-term investors mainly because they often offer:", ["Very short holding periods with quick exits through the stock market", "Daily liquidity on stock exchanges", "Stable, long-dated, sometimes inflation-linked cash flows", "No exposure to regulatory risk"], 2, "Infrastructure assets such as roads and power often have long concessions and contracted revenues, sometimes indexed to inflation. They carry regulatory and construction risk."], ["Introduction to Capital Market Theory", "Beta of a portfolio with 50% in a stock of beta 1.2 and 50% in a stock of beta 0.8 is:", ["2.0", "1.0", "0.96", "0.4"], 1, "Portfolio beta is the weighted average: 0.5 × 1.2 + 0.5 × 0.8 = 1.0."], ["Regulatory Framework", "A Category II AIF with investable funds of ₹320 crore can invest at most how much in one investee company under the general limit?", ["₹32 crore", "₹64 crore", "₹160 crore", "₹80 crore"], 3, "Category I and II AIFs may invest up to 25% of investable funds in one investee: 25% × 320 = ₹80 crore."], ["Fund Due Diligence – Investor Perspective", "A manager shows only its three best deals in a pitch. This is a concern about:", ["Over-diversification across too many sectors in the past funds", "High DPI", "Low fees", "Selective presentation of track record"], 3, "Cherry-picking deals misrepresents overall performance; investors should ask for the full record."], ["Types of Investments", "In a liquidation, which claim is paid first among these?", ["Equity shares", "Senior secured debt", "Preference shares", "Mezzanine debt that is subordinated to senior lenders"], 1, "Senior secured lenders rank ahead of subordinated debt, preference and equity holders, which is why their returns are lower."], ["Taxation", "A non-resident investor in a Category II AIF benefits from a tax treaty by:", ["Opening a fixed deposit", "Registering as an FPI only", "Doing nothing", "Providing a tax residency certificate and forms"], 3, "Treaty rates generally require a tax residency certificate and related forms from the non-resident investor."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "An investor in a close-ended AIF wants to exit early. The most realistic route is:", ["Selling its interest in the secondary market", "Redeeming its units at NAV from the fund on giving notice", "Asking SEBI to refund", "Defaulting on capital calls"], 0, "Close-ended AIFs do not offer redemptions; secondary sales provide liquidity, usually at a discount."], ["Fee Structure and Fund Performance", "Under a deal-by-deal (American) waterfall, the manager:", ["Earns carry only after all capital is returned", "Never earns carry", "Earns carry only on unrealised gains", "Can earn carry on each profitable exit early"], 3, "American waterfalls pay carry deal by deal, so carry can be paid before all capital is returned; a clawback is therefore important."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "A fund that invests in listed shares through preferential allotments and uses derivatives and short positions would most likely be a:", ["Category III AIF", "Category I SME fund", "Category II fund of funds", "Category I social impact fund"], 0, "Strategies involving listed securities with derivatives and shorting fall under Category III."], ["Taxation", "The main purpose of TDS on AIF distributions to residents is to:", ["Replace investors' final tax liability on the income distributed", "Collect tax early and track income", "Exempt investors from filing", "Fund SEBI"], 1, "TDS secures tax revenue and creates an audit trail; investors still declare income and claim credit."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "A right of first refusal (ROFR) in a shareholders' agreement gives the investor:", ["The right to match an offer before shares are sold to a third party", "The right to force all shareholders to sell", "A guaranteed dividend", "Automatic board control"], 0, "ROFR lets an existing holder buy shares on the same terms before they go to an outsider."], ["Taxation", "Carried interest received by managers is a contested tax issue because:", ["It is always exempt", "It may be treated as capital gains or fee income", "It is taxed as agricultural income", "It is outside Indian tax law"], 1, "Characterisation depends on structure and facts; tax authorities may treat it as service income rather than capital gains."], ["Fee Structure and Fund Performance", "A fund has paid-in capital of ₹150 crore, distributions of ₹90 crore and NAV of ₹120 crore. Its TVPI and DPI are:", ["0.6x and 1.4x", "1.4x and 0.8x", "1.4x and 0.6x", "2.1x and 0.6x"], 2, "TVPI = (90 + 120) ÷ 150 = 1.4x. DPI = 90 ÷ 150 = 0.6x."], ["Concept of Informational Efficiency", "'Limits to arbitrage' explain why mispricing can persist because:", ["Arbitrage is always riskless and free", "Regulators ban all arbitrage", "Arbitrage can be costly and risky", "Prices cannot move once set"], 2, "Short-selling constraints, funding costs and the risk that mispricing widens can deter arbitrageurs, so anomalies may persist."], ["AIF Structuring", "In a trust-based AIF, the trust deed is executed between:", ["The investor and the custodian", "The manager and the investee", "The settlor and the trustee", "SEBI and the sponsor"], 2, "The trust deed is executed by the settlor and trustee, creating the trust and setting out its objects."], ["Types of Investments", "A 'payment-in-kind' (PIK) feature in a mezzanine loan means that:", ["Interest is paid in commodities", "The loan is repaid in shares at once", "Interest is added to principal instead of paid in cash", "No interest is ever charged, since the lender is repaid only through equity warrants"], 2, "PIK interest accrues to the principal, conserving the borrower's cash but increasing the lender's risk and final payoff."], ["Investments Landscape", "A family office wants to preserve wealth across generations. Compared with a young salaried saver, it can usually:", ["Hold only cash and deposits because its size makes market investing unnecessary", "Avoid all diversification", "Take more illiquidity risk for higher returns", "Ignore inflation completely"], 2, "With a long horizon and large asset base, a family office can lock up part of its capital to earn the illiquidity premium. It still needs diversification and inflation protection."], ["Valuation", "A company has EBITDA of ₹25 crore. Peers trade at 12x EV/EBITDA and the company's net debt is ₹50 crore. Its equity value is:", ["₹300 crore", "₹350 crore", "₹250 crore", "₹200 crore"], 2, "EV = 25 × 12 = ₹300 crore. Equity = 300 − 50 = ₹250 crore. ₹350 crore wrongly adds net debt."], ["Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory", "Which risk can be reduced by diversification?", ["Systematic market risk", "Risk from economy-wide recessions", "Unsystematic (company-specific) risk", "Changes in the general level of interest rates"], 2, "Company-specific risk can be diversified away. Market-wide risks affect all assets and remain."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "An AIF manager outsources fund administration. Responsibility for compliance with SEBI rules remains with:", ["The manager", "The administrator only", "The investors", "The stock exchange"], 0, "Outsourcing a function does not transfer regulatory responsibility; the manager remains accountable to SEBI and investors for compliance."], ["Indices and Benchmarking", "Why is comparing a PE fund's IRR directly with an index's annual return problematic?", ["IRR is cash-flow weighted while index returns are time-weighted", "Index returns include fees", "IRR always equals index return", "Indices cannot be measured"], 0, "IRR reflects the timing and size of fund cash flows, while index returns assume a buy-and-hold investment, so a like-for-like comparison needs methods such as PME."], ["Fee Structure and Fund Performance", "An investor puts ₹100 crore into a fund and receives ₹144 crore exactly two years later, with no other cash flows. The IRR is:", ["22%", "44%", "18%", "20%"], 3, "IRR solves 100 × (1 + r)² = 144, so r = 20%. 22% is the simple annual average of 44%."], ["Investments Landscape", "The main difference between primary and secondary markets is that in primary markets:", ["Existing securities change hands between investors", "Only derivatives are traded", "Prices are fixed by the regulator", "Issuers raise fresh capital from investors"], 3, "Primary markets involve new issues where money goes to the issuer. Secondary markets provide trading among investors."], ["AIF Structuring", "Separate unit classes for the manager team typically exist to:", ["Avoid reporting the manager team's holdings to SEBI and the trustee", "Increase leverage", "Allocate carry or fee waivers", "Allow early redemption"], 2, "Team classes usually carry carry entitlements and fee waivers, disclosed in the PPM."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "Fund monitoring of portfolio companies typically includes:", ["Daily trading of their shares to capture short-term price moves", "Running their payroll", "Board participation and KPI tracking", "Setting their tax rates"], 2, "Funds monitor portfolio companies through board seats, management reporting and tracking of key performance indicators."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "Which question is most important when judging whether an AIF suits a client?", ["Can she lock in money and bear a total loss?", "Does she prefer to bank with the sponsor's group bank for convenience?", "How many credit cards does she hold?", "What is her favourite sector?"], 0, "Suitability depends on ability to tolerate illiquidity and losses, plus understanding of the product."], ["Fund Due Diligence – Investor Perspective", "Which finding in operational due diligence is a serious concern?", ["Use of a reputed auditor", "A documented valuation policy", "A dedicated compliance officer who reports to the board of the manager", "No independent reconciliation of positions"], 3, "Without independent reconciliation of positions with the custodian and brokers, errors or misappropriation may go undetected."], ["Fund Due Diligence – Investor Perspective", "An investor asks for deal-level attribution of a manager's past funds. The aim is to learn:", ["The location and size of the manager's offices across cities", "The fund's legal form", "Which professionals generated the returns", "The manager's marketing costs"], 2, "Attribution shows who led successful deals and whether they remain with the team."], ["Taxation", "GST on management fees is a cost to investors because:", ["SEBI levies it", "The fund usually cannot claim input credit", "It is refunded to the manager after the fund's annual audit", "It applies only to carry"], 1, "AIFs typically cannot set off GST paid on fees, so it raises fund costs."], ["Fund Due Diligence – Investor Perspective", "A manager's previous fund shows a high IRR but low DPI after eight years. The investor should:", ["Assume the fund is top quartile", "Ignore DPI as irrelevant", "Commit immediately", "Probe why little cash has been returned"], 3, "Low DPI late in a fund's life means value is mostly unrealised, so exit prospects and valuations need scrutiny."], ["AIF Structuring", "A co-investment vehicle allows investors to:", ["Avoid all fund fees permanently on their main fund commitment", "Manage the fund", "Redeem units early", "Invest more in specific deals alongside the fund"], 3, "Co-investment vehicles let selected investors take extra exposure in particular investee companies, under terms set within SEBI's framework."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Material changes to the PPM generally require:", ["Investor consent or an exit option for dissenters", "Only a notice from the manager, with no exit option for investors", "Approval by the investee", "Nothing"], 0, "Material changes require investor approval as prescribed and an exit option for dissenting investors."], ["Investment Strategies, Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "An event-driven strategy in Category III typically profits from:", ["Corporate actions such as mergers and demergers", "Long-term holding of rent-yielding real estate in metro cities", "Collecting coupons on government bonds", "Index tracking"], 0, "Event-driven funds seek gains from price effects of corporate events such as mergers, demergers, buybacks and restructurings."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Large value funds for accredited investors enjoy:", ["Exemption from SEBI registration", "Certain relaxations, such as higher concentration limits", "Guaranteed returns", "Unlimited leverage for Category II schemes investing in unlisted debt"], 1, "SEBI provides relaxations such as higher concentration limits for large value funds of accredited investors."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "Information rights in a PE investment allow the fund to:", ["Vote on all employee hirings", "Receive regular financial and operating reports", "Sell shares without restriction", "Fix product prices"], 1, "Information rights ensure periodic reporting so the investor can monitor its investment."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "Extension of a close-ended Category I or II AIF's tenure beyond its stated life requires:", ["Only the manager's decision", "Approval of two-thirds of unit holders by value, within limits", "SEBI's prior approval for any extension", "No approval at all"], 1, "Tenure may be extended by up to two years with approval of two-thirds of investors by value; further options follow SEBI's framework."], ["AIF Structuring", "An AIF wants to admit foreign investors while keeping its downstream investments classified as domestic under FEMA. The key requirement is that:", ["Foreign investors together hold less than 10% of the scheme's units", "It invests only in listed securities", "Its sponsor and manager are Indian-owned and controlled", "It registers as an FPI"], 2, "Downstream investment is treated as domestic when the sponsor and manager are owned and controlled by resident Indian citizens, regardless of foreign LP share."], ["Introduction to Capital Market Theory", "A stock plots above the security market line. This suggests it is:", ["Overvalued, since it offers less than its required return", "Undervalued, offering more than its required return", "Risk-free", "Perfectly priced"], 1, "A security above the SML offers a higher expected return than CAPM requires for its beta, implying undervaluation."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "A recapitalisation as an exit route means the portfolio company:", ["Lists on a stock exchange", "Raises debt to pay a dividend to shareholders", "Is liquidated", "Merges with the fund so that its shares are cancelled"], 1, "In a dividend recap, the company borrows and distributes the proceeds, allowing a partial exit without selling shares."]]};
